{"step_index":140,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T08:59:37Z","model":"gemini-3.8-flash-high","input_tokens":3050,"cache_read_tokens":130351,"output_tokens":2846,"thinking":"The codebase appears to have large CSS files, specifically `luxury-pro.css`, `home-upgrade.css`, and `header-pro.css`, each exceeding 1000 lines. The `server.js` file serves frontend assets, so the size of these CSS files could impact performance.\n\n\nThe `server.js` file sets aggressive caching in production but disables it during development, which results in poor local testing performance due to excessive HTTP requests. The current configuration uses gzip compression but doesn't implement Brotli, even though the current Node.js version supports Brotli compression which could improve performance further.\n\n\nThe `server.js` file currently compresses assets on-the-fly with gzip, leading to CPU spikes and increased time-to-first-byte (TTFB), as compression isn't cached. Caching the compressed buffer, or using an LRU cache, would allow for instant response times. JavaScript file `quick-actions.js` immediately schedules the download and execution of the 212KB `kezza-ai.min.js` file, which blocks the main thread.\n\n\nThe `kezza-ai.min.js` file, which blocks the main thread, should be loaded lazily to improve initial page load performance. It can be deferred until the first user interaction (mouse movement, touch, scroll, keypress, or click on a chat button) or scheduled with `requestIdleCallback` after an initial delay. The 3D logo animation in `scroll-3d.js` continuously triggers expensive layer promotion, 3D matrix recalculations, and an infinite animation loop from the moment the page starts loading.\n\n\nThe 3D logo animation's resource-intensive computations at startup are unnecessary; delaying their execution improves initial page rendering speed. The `pro-animations.js` file contains an extensive series of DOM queries and class manipulations performed on `DOMContentLoaded`, which, if delayed, could improve perceived responsiveness. Applying `requestIdleCallback`, `window.onload` or first user scroll for `scroll-3d` seems like a solid solution.\n\n\nThe code performs numerous DOM queries on page load, causing excessive style recalculations and reflows by querying elements with class names such as `sm-tab-panel` and attaching mousemove listeners. Several DOM element selections and `getBoundingClientRect` calls trigger forced reflows within mouseenter and mousemove event handlers. Continuous scroll event listeners are also attached, which, combined with the initial reflow and style recalculation, suggest performance bottlenecks.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"grep -n \"testiGrid\" frontend/index.html","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Find testiGrid in index.html"}}]}
