{"step_index":148,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-15T08:59:57Z","model":"gemini-3.8-flash-high","input_tokens":5765,"cache_read_tokens":134422,"output_tokens":2443,"thinking":"Initial inspection reveals `upgrades-pro.css` styles key landing page elements. Bundling or streamlining `genuine-reviews.css`, `site-quality.css`, and `upgrades-pro.css` could optimize CSS loading. Investigating `server.js`'s static file serving and compression logic is the next step to confirm this.\n\n\nInspecting the server's response handling reveals on-the-fly gzip compression, but it appears to have a bypass for empty or non-200 responses, as well as those already encoded. Compression is skipped if content is empty or server status is not 200. This could indicate a missed optimization opportunity if static assets aren't pre-compressed or if status codes are inconsistent.\n\n\nThe compression middleware repeatedly compresses the same static assets on each request without caching, leading to unnecessary CPU load. The gzip compression uses an older, less efficient algorithm (zlib) when brotli compression is available and faster. Moreover, the server's cache control settings are overly aggressive, preventing the browser from caching assets in non-production environments. Addressing this could significantly improve initial load times and responsiveness.\n\n\nServer optimization will prioritize pre-compressed static asset caching in memory, supporting both gzip and Brotli encoding for efficient delivery. Caching strategies will be updated to include more intelligent Cache-Control headers, including immutable for versioned assets and stale-while-revalidate for unversioned files. Render-blocking resources in the HTML head will be addressed by deferring a JavaScript file and asynchronously loading a critical CSS subset.\n\n\nRemoved the redundant Google Fonts preload, aligning with the existing `@font-face` approach in CSS and enabling system fallbacks. Deferred Google Tag Manager and Google Analytics using `requestIdleCallback` to prevent blocking the initial paint.\n\nLazy-loaded `kezza-ai.min.js` and review fetching. The former now loads on user interaction or idle time, removing it from the critical render path. The latter's loading is now triggered by `IntersectionObserver` when the testimonials section comes into view.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":55,"Path":"/Users/ravi/Desktop/kezza_clinic-main/frontend/js/quick-actions.js","StartLine":15}}]}
